```
  ECF_ref notes "ECF value is calculated based on body weight and sex."
  vout_h2o notes ```
The water lost without urine: insensible through the skin and thelungs (about 700
ml a day), the sweat and the feces (about 100 mleach) [Hall2021, Guyton and Hall
Textbook of Medical Physiology,chapter 25]. Of the intake of 2.3 l a day the urine
is 1.4 l atthe reference, the 1.47 to 1.96 l a day of Jeon2012 and Dussol2005;without
the loss the urine was the whole intake.
```
  Vloop notes ```
The fluid of the renal tubule, lumped from the glomerulus to theurine: the filtered
sodium, chloride and water are reabsorbed fromit or excreted into the urine. HCTZ
increases the excretion ofsodium and chloride, by its inhibition of the sodium chloridecotransporter
in the distal convoluted tubule, a part of thetubule. The id `Vloop` is historical, the
compartment is not theloop of Henle.
```
  diuresis notes "Excretion rate water urine."
  BW notes "Reference adult body weight (Ogden2004, Jones2013, Thompson2009, Brown1997)."
  FVgu notes "Fractional volume of the gut (Brown1997, Jones2013, Thompson2009)."
  FVki notes "Fractional volume of the kidneys (Brown1997, Jones2013, Thompson2009)."
  FVli notes "Fractional volume of the liver (Wynne1989, Brown1997, Jones2013,Thompson2009)."
  FVlu notes "Fractional volume of the lungs (Brown1997, Jones2013, Thompson2009)."
  FVve notes "Fractional volume of the venous blood (Brown1997, Jones2013, Thompson2009)."
  FVar notes "Fractional volume of the arterial blood (Brown1997, Jones2013,Thompson2009)."
  Fblood notes ```
The vascular compartments are whole blood: the arterial and thevenous blood, the
portal and the hepatic vein and the blood of thetissues (`V*_plasma`). The flows are blood
flows, and the speciesof the vascular compartments (named plasma) are the concentrationin
plasma with a blood to plasma ratio of 1, which is what thedata of plasma are compared
with. An erythrocyte uptake of HCTZ(blood to plasma ratio above 1) is not modelled.
The value 0.02 isan assumption of the model without a source; the vascularfraction
of the kidney and the liver is about ten times larger(ICRP 89, Valentin2002).
```
  Vgu_lumen notes ```
The volume the concentration of hydrochlorothiazide in the lumen ofthe intestine
(`Cgu_hctz`) refers to; it is the volume of the gut`Vgu`, the model has no volume of the lumen
of its own.
```
  FVpo notes "Small volume of the portal vein, an assumption of the model without a source."
  FVhv notes "Small volume of the hepatic vein, an assumption of the model without a source."
  hctz notes ```
This should be the concentration in the kidney, but no kidney compartment in the
fluid model.
```
  na notes "Concentration in mM corresponding to mEq/l."
  na_ref notes "reference sodium concentration."
  na_loop notes "Concentration in mM corresponding to mEq/l."
  na_urine notes "Concentration in mM corresponding to mEq/l."
  cl notes "Concentration in mM corresponding to mEq/l."
  cl_ref notes "reference chloride concentration."
  cl_loop notes "Concentration in mM corresponding to mEq/l."
  cl_urine notes "Concentration in mM corresponding to mEq/l."
  f_TBW notes ```
0.55 l/kg, between the typical values of men and women:male: 0.6 l/kgfemale: 0.5
l/kg
```
  f_ECF notes "Around 1/3 of total body water (TBW) is extracellular fluid (ECF)."
  Psys_ref notes ```
Psys value is dependent on sex and age.


 
 Young Adult Men (18-39 years): Average=120/80 mmHg
 Middle-aged Men (40-59 years): Average=125−130/80−85 mmHg
 Older Men (60+ years): Average=135−140/85−90 mmHg

 Young Adult Women (18-39 years): Average=110−120/70−80mmHg
 Middle-aged Women (40-59 years): Average=120−130/80−85mmHg
 Older Women (60+ years): Average=135−140/85−90mmHg
```
  Pdia_ref notes ```
Pdia value is dependent on sex and age.


 
 Young Adult Men (18-39 years): Average=120/80 mmHg
 Middle-aged Men (40-59 years): Average=125−130/80−85 mmHg
 Older Men (60+ years): Average=135−140/85−90 mmHg

 Young Adult Women (18-39 years): Average=110−120/70−80mmHg
 Middle-aged Women (40-59 years): Average=120−130/80−85mmHg
 Older Women (60+ years): Average=135−140/85−90mmHg
```
  k_na notes "Excretion rate sodium urine."
  k_cl notes "Excretion rate chloride urine."
  k_h2o notes "Excretion rate water urine."
  counter_gamma notes "Counter-regulation feedback."
  f_h2o_max notes ```
The urine is at most this fraction of the filtration, the rest ofthe filtered water
is reabsorbed. Without the limit an expandedextracellular fluid (twice its reference)
with HCTZ excretes morewater than is filtered: the reabsorption of water and sodium
turnsnegative and the integration fails. Within the supported domain thefractional
excretion of water stays below 0.09, the limit is notreached.
```
  E50_hctz_nacl notes "E50 for HCTZ effect on sodium and chloride excretion."
  Emax_hctz_na notes "Emax for HCTZ effect on sodium excretion."
  Emax_hctz_cl notes "Emax for HCTZ effect on chloride excretion."
  BW_ref notes ```
Body weight at which the exchange flows of the tissue distribution (`ftissue_<sid>`) are given;
they scale with the body weight as the volumes, the blood flows and the renal clearance
do.
```
  f_renal_function notes ```
>
> 1.0: increased kidney function
> 1.0: normal kidney function (healthy control)
> <1.0: decreased kidney function
>
> 
Renal function relative to the healthy glomerular filtrationrate GFR_base, which
is 101 ml/min at 75 kg; a measuredclearance of X ml/min is the renal function X/101.

Supported range 0.05 to 2, a GFR of 5 to 202 ml/min. The renalperfusion does not
change with the renal function: the GFR isf_renal_function \* 0.0763 of the renal
blood flow `Qki` (themodel has no hematocrit; with a hematocrit of 0.45 it isf_renal_function
\* 0.139 of the renal plasma flow, a filtrationfraction of 0.28 at 2). Below 0.05
the urine flow approaches 0and the concentration of HCTZ in the urine is not defined.
```
  f_GFR_Qki notes ```
GFR per renal blood flow (76.3 ml/l, a ratio of 0.0763). Calibratedso that the healthy
GFR is 101 ml/min at the renal blood flow of1.32354 l/min of 75 kg, the normal GFR
of the classes of renalimpairment.
```
  f_cardiac_function notes "Factor scaling cardiac output in cardiac impairment."
  COBW notes ```
Cardiac output per body weight of healthy adults, about 6.5l/min at 70 kg (Cattermole2017,
Patel2021, Collis2001).
```
  FQgu notes ```
Assumed FQgu is the sum of FQgu + FQsp + FQpa for correct portal blood flow.with
FQgu=0.146, FQsp=0.017, FQpa=0.017 (Brown1997, Jones2013, Thompson2009).
```
  FQki notes "Fraction of the cardiac output to the kidneys (Brown1997,Jones2013, Thompson2009)."
  FQh notes ```
Fraction of the cardiac output leaving the liver, the portal(FQgu) and the hepatic
arterial flow (Wynne1989, Brown1997,Jones2013, Thompson2009). The hepatic artery
is 3.5 % of thecardiac output here, about 6.5 % in ICRP 89 (Valentin2002);without
a hepatic clearance of HCTZ the difference does notchange the pharmacokinetics.
```
  f_cirrhosis notes ```
Cirrhosis is not supported for HCTZ: it changes only the shuntsand the liver tissue,
HCTZ has no hepatic clearance (the AUCchanges by 0.03 % at 0.9); ascites, the expansion
of theextracellular fluid and aldosterone are not in the model.
```
  GU__application_hctz notes "The rate at which the oral dose `PODOSE_hctz` enters the stomach(`POSTOMACH_hctz`)."
  GU__application_solution_hctz notes "The oral dose given as a solution enters the lumen of the intestinewithout dissolution."
  KI__HCTZEX_f notes ```
The intrinsic renal clearance of HCTZ is HCTZEX_f times the GFR(filtration and secretion),
acting on the renal venous plasma of awell-stirred kidney: the systemic renal clearance
isQki \* CLint/(Qki + CLint), limited by the renal blood flow (2.26GFR at renal function
1 with HCTZEX_f 2.68, 2.63 at 0.1 and 1.96at 2). It scales with the body weight as
the GFR does (linearly).
```
  ftissue_hctz notes "Exchange flow at the reference body weight `BW_ref`."
  GU__F_hctz_abs notes ```
Fraction absorbed, i.e., only a fraction of the hydrochlorothiazide in the intestinal
lumenis absorbed. This parameter determines how much of the hydrochlorothiazide is
excreted.

`F_hctz_abs` of dose is absorbed. `(1-F_hctz_abs)` is excreted in feces.

around 11-25% of dose recovered in feces {Beermann1976}around 60-70 percent urinary
recovery of oral dose~70-80 percent urinary recovery of oral dose [Barbhaiya1982]~70
percent after oral dose, ~93% IV dose [Beermann1976]
```
  GU__dissolution_hctz notes "Dissolution of the dose in the stomach and its passage into thelumen of the intestine."
  GU__Ka_application_hctz notes "Fast application to shift applied dose in the stomach."
  NA_UPTAKE notes "Na uptake increased when concentrations too low."
  CL_UPTAKE notes "Cl uptake increased when concentrations too low."

